  "headline_finding": "Complete ranking inversion under execution. On static metrics (dissertation ch.5) the fine-tuned models beat both commercial baselines on every measure without exception; under genuine execution against live applications the baselines pass 42/96 specs fully (43.8%) versus 2/96 (2.1%) for the fine-tuned systems. Root cause: fine-tuned models impose training-corpus conventions (data-testid selectors, invented routes, fabricated intercepts) over the explicit real selectors stated in each story, while zero-shot baselines follow the stated selectors. Static quality and execution readiness are anti-correlated in this setting; grounding, not framework fluency, is the binding constraint. The fine-tuned playwright combos were re-executed as .spec.ts to rule out harness TS-syntax artifacts; results were identical."
